From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] chpass: the FreeBSD file path, with pw_util's record functions

Apple's chpass reads and writes the account through Open Directory and
CoreFoundation (open_directory.c), both closed. Its sources keep FreeBSD's
path under !__APPLE__: edit the record, then pw_copy() the changed line
into a copy of /etc/master.passwd and run pwd_mkdb -p, which installs it
and writes /etc/passwd. With PW_FILES, NeoDarwin's switch (without it
upstream is unchanged), chpass builds that path:
 - every #ifdef __APPLE__ in chpass/ becomes
   defined(__APPLE__) && !defined(PW_FILES), and #ifndef __APPLE__ its
   negation; util.c's Darwin includes stay (tzfile.h's TM_YEAR_BASE);
 - FreeBSD's <pw_scan.h> and <libutil.h> are pwd_mkdb's pw_scan.h and
   vipw's pw_util.h, which declares the record functions (pw_copy, pw_dup,
   pw_equal, pw_make, pw_scan, pw_tempname) and PWSCAN_*;
 - vipw/pw_util.c builds those functions, which it keeps under
   !__APPLE__, with PW_FILES; Darwin's reallocarray is libmalloc's
   ($DARWIN_EXTSN, not in the SDK), so pw_copy() doubles its buffer with
   realloc;
 - Darwin's struct passwd has no pw_fields, so the record's source is
   always the files.
chpass, chfn and chsh are installed setuid root, as FreeBSD's are: a user
changes their own shell and finger information after giving their password,
and root changes any record.

